				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><span style="font-size: 18px; line-height: normal"><span style="font-weight: bold">Rathe Mountains</span></span>
<br />

<br />

<br />
Prelude
<br />

<br />

<br />
“Up you go lass.”
<br />

<br />
Devin MacTarin grasped Bridgid about the waist, lifted her up on the oxcart and noticed her pregnant belly had grown.  In the week since they'd left High Keep she'd put on a lot of weight although she was still a fine looking woman.  So fine looking with her admirable bosom and delicate traces of elven features he found it difficult to behave with any type of discipline.  He click the reins on the oxen and tried not to look at her. All he really wanted to do was hold her in his arms and hear her moan as she had back in the room in high keep.  He wanted her to teach him exotic pleasures, squeeze him and kiss him till they were both breathless and straining for satisfaction.   He wanted all those things and he had to content himself with concealed glances and not so casual brushes of skin as they crossed the plains of East Karana.  It was far too dangerous to stop for carnal pleasures and he knew Bridgid wasn't in the mood.   As soon as they got to his home in Surefall, matters were going to change.  Sooner if he could manage it.   He planned on a quick marriage and a feast with his family to celebrate not only his bringing home a bride but the birth of his first born.
<br />

<br />
He took her delicate hand in his palm.  “Are you feeling well, m'dear.”
<br />

<br />
“I feel wonderful.  So well I could walk all the way to Qeynos.” she replied as she pulled her hand out of his and took an unusual interest in the tall prairie grasses.
<br />

<br />
“Good idea. I'm sure the oxen could use a break.”  He replied in a joke that fell flat.  He slanted his gaze and watched her.  For all her sarcasm he was growing worried about her.  Her complaints didn't have the edge he'd grown used to.  She was wilting.  Ever since the trouble had started in High Keep Bridgid hadn't been herself.   He sighed.  He supposed he couldn't blame her.  Two half elven guards were murdered while patrolling the Kithicor borders.  Witnesses said the killers had been a hoard of Tier'dal sporting red satin sashes.   Not only had they killed the guards but they'd cut off their heads and hands and took the body parts with them.   Since then, High Keep had closed it's doors to dark elves, even the semi-friendly ones that sometimes visited. 
<br />

<br />
This had Bridgid very worried.  Her father  had been a Tier'dal and traces of her dark ancestry were reflected in her features.  Her hair was naturally snowy but she dyed it blond and her brows were arched like the Tier'dal females.  Devin assured her that the guards wouldn't force her out,  but he'd been wrong.  A vote had been taken in the dead of one night and the other citizens of the keep wanted her gone.   She was one of the the darkies, they said. He tried to convince them they were wrong.  But Bridgid had always been a little too candid about her origins.   She would brag about how her mother fell for a dark elf lord when she performed one of her many erotic her songs.    Her stage antics made her very popular with the guards but not with their wives.  And in this case the voice of the wives was loud.  
<br />

<br />
Devin's cries that Brigid would never harm any of them fell on deaf ears.   Even though she was heavy with child, ready to deliver at any time, the citizens were adamant. What if the baby was born an inkie.  It could happen, they'd said.  They wanted her either gone or locked up.   They thought he was a fool for defending her.  He decided they were jealous that she agreed to marry him if he offered her protection.   Which was why he made the decision to resign his post, leave High Keep,  and head for home.  He wanted her to have the baby in the comfort and safety of Surefall surrounded by his family.
<br />

<br />
It had been five years since he'd  set out from Surefall and he'd never made the crossing through the Karanas.  A druid had teleported himself and several friends to Western Commons when they'd first set out on adventures.  Unfortunately some trouble in the high Keep had forced them to take positions as guards till they'd worked off their debts, but once his debt was paid he'd stayed mainly because of Bridgid.  He'd been in love with her from the moment he set eyes on her.    The way she moved when she sang had him fascinated and he'd spend every evening he could looking at her.  His friends had all since moved on from High Keep but he had stayed and now he couldn't believe he was actually taking her home to Surefall.
<br />

<br />
Of course what the people of Surefall called civilization probably looked like barbaric to a girl raised in Freeport.  A few huts huddled together on the shore of a lake, and the farms were few and far between.  Most of the folk of the glade lived out in the woods away from each other and only came to town for necessary supplies.   Still there were more folk in the glade than out here in the Karanas.  In the week they'd been crossing East Karana they'd seen next to no one.   The first four nights they'd slept out in open air on the long winding mountainous road down.  The fifth night they'd curled up in the wagon, on the edge of an abandoned barbarian outpost, and last night they'd stumbled upon a small farm with a  cottage, barn and fields.  He'd bartered with the farmer to stay in his barn and with the farmers wife for two bowls of thick sheep stew and two tankards of ale.  Bridgid had eaten as if she'd never had such fine fare and had slept through the night content on a bale of  hay with a roof over her head.  
<br />

<br />
But now, as the eve of the seventh day drew close Devin caught the scent of water and the change in the wind.  More than that he spotted a wide path deep with wagon ruts.  They neared the river and the bridge to the Northern plains of Karana.    He drew up the reins on the oxen as an old knight and his squire rode up.  He was surprised to see a patrol so far from the High Keep.
<br />

<br />
“Where you folk headed?” the old Knight asked.
<br />

<br />
“To Surefall Glade, Sire.” Devin answered as he put an arm around Bridgid.
<br />

<br />
“Long road you have ahead and more danger than usual.  Don't supposed I could convince you to go back?”
<br />

<br />
“This missus is with child and we wanted to have it with the family.” Devin answered nodding at Bridgid.
<br />

<br />
The knight ran his eyes over Bridgid's Tier'dal features and swollen midsection.  He looked grim.   “Be careful and stay on the main road.  Don't sleep out in the open.  There's more danger than usual on the open plains.”
<br />

<br />
“Danger?  What sort of danger.” Devin asked.
<br />

<br />
“Dark elves.  They usually come out at night.  Make sure when the sun goes down you find cover.”
<br />

<br />
“I thank you for the warning.  Might you know where we could find shelter when we cross over?”.  
<br />

<br />
The knight looked thoughtful.  “The gypsies of the Combine are the closest to the bridge but I wouldn't fully trust them.  You'd be better off passing the gypsies and heading south towards the druids.  Mayhap you might even catch a ride straight to Surefall if one of the Elders pass through.”
<br />

<br />
Thanking the knight for the information, Devin clicked the reins and headed towards the long rickety bridge.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br />Kaldarye Ka'Duceus
