				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">The northern side of the bridge looked exactly like East Karana.  A plain of yellow grasses stretched as far as the eye could see but here the sounds were different.   Grifawns cried and lions growled.  Devil pulled the wagon off the road and went down to the river to refill his water jugs before the began the next leg of their journey.  Bridgid did not follow.
<br />

<br />
He turned and looked back at her.  “Come walk with me, love,  and stretch your legs.”
<br />

<br />
Her head snapped up.  “Only if you promise me we'll stop somewhere that I can get a bath.”
<br />

<br />
“Darling we're in the plains of North Karana, there isn't anyplace to get a bath here.”
<br />

<br />
“The river.” she said pointing at the water as she climbed off of the wagon. “I can bathe in the river.”
<br />

<br />
He waited for her to catch up to him, steadying her with his palm under her elbow as they walked down the slippery bank.  She leaned against him for support and he could smell the scent of her lingering against him  .“Don't go in too deep.  I don't know how strong the current is or if there are any critters swimming in the water.” he said as he released her near a cluster of rocks that formed a small tributary.  She sat and pulled off her short leather boots and tested the water with her toes before lowering her entire foot into it.
<br />

<br />
“Why are you staring at me?” She said after she'd lowered most of her legs into the water.
<br />

<br />
He blushed.  He hadn't realized he'd been staring.  Staring at her dusky shoulders, bleached hair, tapered thighs, cat-shaped eyes, slightly pointed ears, and the mystique of the Tier'dal in her features.  “I just like the way you look.” he replied.
<br />

<br />
With a smile composed in equal parts, seduction, invitation and passion she patted the water with a gentle splash and replied, “Come into the river, the water feels good.” 
<br />

<br />
They stayed in the river much too long but Devin had enjoyed every minute of it. When they finally emerged from the water, the stars were blinking overhead in a sky so vast that he thought maybe he died and went heaven.    Bridgid shivered as she huddled close.   Enjoying the serenity of the night and majesty of the Karana's they got dressed and walked back to the oxcart just over the hill.   Devin felt as if life could never get more perfect.  But when they crested the hill the illusion of serenity vanished.  The oxen were gone as were all their mutual belongings.    Devin couldn't believe the oxen had wandered off on him.  He glanced down at Bridgid as she stared at the empty plain without expression.   
<br />

<br />
“The oxen wandered.  I forgot to tie them.  I didn't  think we'd be gone so long.” he said sheepishly.
<br />

<br />
“What are we going to do.” she said as she sank to the ground.  She began to grasp clods of dirt and crumble them in her fingers.
<br />

<br />
“We're going to find them.” Devin replied putting his hands under her arms and helping her back to her feet.  “Come on love, they can't have wandered far.  They are lugging around a heavy wagon.”  He couldn't believe he had been so stupid but now wasn't the time to berate himself.  They had to find that cart if they were going to complete their journey.  He inspected the ground where he had left them and followed the ruts of the cart in the earth.  Tracking was never one of his strong points and he tried to remember the lessons from his youth that his uncle Zuljan had attempted to instill.  “You could be a good ranger, Dev, if you'd just pay attention.” his uncle had scolded.  Now he desperately wished he'd been a better student, but back in his youth he never dreamed he'd one day find himself in a situation like this where his life would depend on his ability to follow the tracks of a wagon.
<br />

<br />
Bridgid tried to remove her arm and shrink back to the ground but he held fast and pulled her against him, shielding her from the rising wind with his cloak.  “Come, love, it won't be long.  We'll find the oxen and tether them down by the river then I'll build you a fire.”
<br />

<br />
“I'm hungry.” she said dully as she allowed herself to be pulled along.
<br />

<br />
He kept talking, more from nervous energy than anything else as they followed the trail under Karana's night.   “Do you see that bright star up there?”  he said pointing to a brilliant blue twinkle.  When she didn't respond he continued.  “That's Endah, the ranger's guide.  It always points to the north.  If we follow it, we'll go right back to High Keep.”  
<br />

<br />
He glanced at her.  Her face looked drawn and her eyes were closed.  Her legs moved slowly.  Someday they'd probably look back on this night and laugh but right now he was worried.  Her belly seemed lower as if the child were bearing it's weight on her hips.  
<br />

<br />
“The name Endah sounds Elven.” she said.  
<br />

<br />
“It is.” he continued.  “The real Endah was a wood elf who accidentally became a sailor, on the frozen north seas.  He'd been kidnapped by gnome pirates but got marooned in the land of dragons far away in Western Wastes.”  He squeezed her hand.  “You think we have problems, Endah  found himself trapped in the land where Dragons nested. The gnomes would send him into the wasteland without protection to forage food and one day he didn't return.  He'd found an icy cave populated by beautiful siren's and traded one set of captors for another.”
<br />

<br />
She opened her eyes and looked at him doubtfully.  “What happened to the gnomes.  Why didn't they look for him?”
<br />

<br />
Devin shrugged.  “No one knows.  It's said the gnomes are stuck there still, on a broken ship, surrounded by the ice and great wurms to this very day.”
<br />

<br />
“And the ranger?”
<br />

<br />
Devin grinned.  “Legend has it eventually he found a secret exit out of the siren's cave but it was underwater and when he surfaced he was in the sea.  Rather than return to the siren's cavern he swam till he reached a frozen sandy shore.   There was a druid portal hidden there amongst the rocks and eventually a druidess ported in and rescued him”
<br />

<br />
“A druidess, hah.  I suppose she was beautiful.”
<br />

<br />
“Very.  There's another story about her in the legend as well.”
<br />

<br />
Bridgid kicked at the ground.  “So which star is the druidess.”
<br />

<br />
“That one.” he said pointing to a reddish star near the bright blue one.  “Her name is Justica.  That isn't really a star though, it's a comet.  It travels across the skies depending on the season.  Druids are wanderers, so I guess naming them after a star is difficult since they can't be pinned down.” he laughed and squeezed her arm.
<br />

<br />
“You should have been a bard.” Bridgid said as she snuggled against him.  “you tell a good tale.”  
<br />

<br />
Devin scoffed.  “I haven't the talent for music, and besides, my uncle gave me instruction as a ranger, though that never really interested me.  But all my family are rangers.  You'll be the first that will be different.”
<br />

<br />
“Do you think they'll care?  You're family.”
<br />

<br />
“Nay.  They'll be thrilled to have you.  It's been a long time since there's been a babe with the MacTarin family name about the glade.”
<br />

<br />
She sighed and put a hand on her swollen belly.  “I'm nervous having it.  I heard the pain is bad.”
<br />

<br />
“Don't worry.  We have the best shaman from Halas in all the land that will attend you.  It will go easy.  He delivered both me and my sister from my mother, and delivers all the babies from Surefall to the gates of Qeynos.” 
<br />

<br />
Looking relieved she nodded, then  stumbed on the uneven terrain.  “My legs hurt.” she said.
<br />

<br />
Devin caught her, steadied her then checked again for evidence of the Oxen.  There was some sort of disturbance in the ground that he couldn't make out.  At first he feared lions but there was no blood.  Just a lot of trampled grass.  It was strange though.  The oxen had changed direction and headed down closer to the river.  Perhaps they were thirsty he reasoned as he helped Bridgid back down the slippery bank.  He wondered how the oxen had managed the softer ground on their own without getting stuck.
<br />

<br />
“They can't be too much further ahead.” he said as he caught her before she fell.  The ground is too soft for them to run.  “Do you see them with your vision love?” he asked her, knowing she had superior night sight with her half dark-elven eyes.
<br />

<br />
She squinted in the distance.  “Yes I see them, but only one of them.  The other seems to have gotten loose.”
<br />

<br />
He pulled along and tried not to let his anxiety show.  He had no idea how one of the oxen could get away. He knew he'd secured their harnesses.   As they got closer to where the ox cart waited, the smell of cooking meat and men carousing wafted on the wind.  He thought about having Bridgid wait by a clump of rocks out of sight when a voice in the darkness stopped him.
<br />

<br />
“Well, well well, what 'ave we here.” a thickly accented voice said.
<br />

<br />
“Looks like a pair of star struck lovers walking a moonlit beach.” another deeper gruff voice added.
<br />

<br />
“Your a wee bit far from Qeynos.” the first voice said in the darkness.
<br />

<br />
“Show yourself.”Devin said as he spun towards the voices.
<br />

<br />
Besides him Bridgid stiffened and whispered. “Humans.”
<br />

<br />
Devin relaxed.   He had feared the voices belonged to dark elves.  At least humans could be reasonable.  “My name is Devin MacTarin from Surefall Glade and I think you and your companions have found my ox cart.
<br />

<br />
“Surefall Glade?”
<br />

<br />
“Your oxcart?”
<br />

<br />
“You mean our oxcart.  Our dinner.” the gruff voice said punching Devin in the gut.
<br />

<br />
Ten minutes later, tied up and sporting several bruises to his face Devin revised his 'reasonable' opinion of the humans.  Especially since they were eating one of his oxen and drawing lots deciding who would get first round with Bridgid.  <span style="font-style: italic">His Bridgid</span>.  He glanced at her.  She sat tied up to the wheel of the cart looking terrified.  Her eyes were closed and swollen with tears.   He tried to remember what the Brave Endah had done when he'd first been captured but somehow gnomes and humans seemed different.  
<br />

<br />
“It's ok Bridgid, I'll get us out of here.” he whispered as he frantically tried to free himself from the binds.  He had no idea what he was doing and his wrists were rubbing raw from his efforts.  The last time his hands had been shackled was back in High Keep when he and his friends had first gotten into trouble. Those bindings had been chains not rope and they'd been placed in dark dirty cells with no hope of escape.  At least the folk of High Keep had offered them a way out of their predicament.  Here he didn't think there'd be a benevolent judge looking for more bodies to fill the garrison.
<br />

<br />
“No talking.” the leader of the brigands said as he walked over to them. He chewed on a haunch of meat and gave a leering look at Bridgid.  “Serve us well wench and maybe we'll feed you and your lover.” he said passing the burnt ox flesh in front of her eyes.  
<br />

<br />
Bridgid glared at him and spat.  Causing the bandit to erupt in a fit of laughter.  We got ourselves a feisty one, boys.” he said as he wiped her spittle off his face.  “It's going to be a grand evening.” The brigand turned away from Bridgid and struck Devin.  
<br />

<br />
Devin curled over as his breath left his lungs.  The brigand punched him again, and because his hands were tied behind him he was helpless to defend.  He screamed and  looked at Bridgid with fear in his eyes trying to will her to be brave as pain and unconsciousness overwhelmed him.  
<br />

<br />
The brigand continued pummeling Devin with his fists despite Bridgid's protests.  When he was finished he faced her and said, “You spit on me again, wench, and I'll kill him and leave his body for the ghouls.”
<br />

<br />
“Why are you doing this.  Why are you so evil.”
<br />

<br />
He laughed.   “Evil.  We're not evil.  There's worse out here on the plains of Karana than us brigands.”
<br />

<br />
Bridgid stared at him terrified. He cupped her chin with his hands and started stroking her face.  He slid one hand up her dress.  She didn't resist when he put his hairy lips on her mouth.  She didn't resist as he ran his greasy fingers down her bosom.   She didn't make a sound either when his body sagged against hers and the point of a blade that impaled his neck and heart poked out of his ribcage dangerously close to her pregnant belly.  The bandit's blood spurted and ran down the front of her dress as  he  convulsed in swift death.  
<br />

<br />
Around her more bandits were screaming as the twang of bows crackled the air.  Two more dropped in front of her fatally wounded by arrows, but she only had eyes for the new threat in front of her.  The dark elf on the other side of the blade that killed the lead bandit was sizing her up.   His eyes were bright amber and he moved like a dancer.   His skin was so black that he blended with the night and she had to look at him with infravision.  He pulled the blade out of the brigand and let the corpse fall against her.  Then he kicked the body away.   
<br />

<br />
She stared at him frozen as he was joined by several companions.  They pointed at her and spoke in low voices in a language she could barely understand but one she recognized.  They were speaking Tier'dal.  She remembered her mother using a few phrases of it back in Freeport.  That was before her mother had disappeared.  Was she now about to face the same fate.  She hoped she was like her mother and too attractive for them to slay her outright.
<br />
 
<br />
Luckily they must have agreed for they didn't harm her, instead they untied her from the wheel and put her in the back of the wagon where they retied her arms.  Then looked at Devin.  They conferred again, checked to see if he was breathing, and slashed his bindings and rolled him over, speaking to each other in their strange melodic voices.  
<br />

<br />
Bridgit had never thought so before but listening to Tier'dal was like listening to someone who had lost a great love.  It was mournful and beautiful and lonely.  One of the dark elves kicked Devin causing him to groan then they drug him into the cart.  Devin collapsed next to her blissfully unaware of the danger.   
<br />

<br />
The Tier'dal searched the corpses of the brigands before smacking the remaining ox into motion. They moved out of the bandit camp with great speed.  Bridgid wasn't aware an ox could move so fast as they headed deeper into the Karana's towards the sound of roaring lions.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br />Kaldarye Ka'Duceus
