				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">-End of Prelude-
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
Half a mile into Rathe mountains direction sense becomes non-existent for the inexperienced traveler.  Though serenely beautiful the mountains were deadly.  High sharp peaks laid against one another more perfectly arranged than any artist's canvass.  The lower hills were hunter green, the upper bluish gray with white glacial snowcaps.   In the distance the peaks faded to purple against the still blue sky.  But hidden between the majestic grandeur of the mountains were chutes, steep and deadly, dropping down sometimes over a mile to scrub valleys.  Cyclops, giants, undead and drakes populated these valleys and the few sentient folk that eeked out a living in the  remote hills were suspicious of strangers.  
<br />

<br />
It had been a long time since Vilamil had visited this part of Norrath.  His memories of  Rathe were mostly of chasing the inhabitants of hate in the dead of the night when a jagged hole had been ripped in the fabric of the planes and Innoruuk's minions had spilled into these mountains.  The jagged peaks still showed scars of deadly battles once fought, but in the daylight everything looked different.  Vilamil wasted most of the day trying to locate the tier'dal outpost.  The sun was wavering in the purplish sky, sinking like a red hot ball of dying light when he finally came upon the old fortress.
<br />

<br />
He felt weary and his feet hurt. The stone keep in Rathe Mountains looked run down as if it once tested a dragon's might and fared poorly.  A deep sooty scar blackened one crumbling wall, and the earth surrounding that wall was bare of vegetation.  Two of the three towers were choking in creeping vines while the third stood starkly alone, in good repair with it's battlements and crenolations untouched.  At the top of this tower stood the flag of Neriak and mounted on the side was the seal of king  Thex.    Vilamil trekked down the stone path leading towards the fortress noting that its  fence was in sore need of repair.  Half of it was crumbling the other half had been partially rebuilt using crudely hewn granite.  A few patrols prowled the grounds and nearby foothills on scaly drogmar.  Overall this Tier'dal haven did not look inviting but neither did the thought of sleeping outside in the open air of Rathe Mountains.  
<br />

<br />
They were suspicious of him when he asked for shelter but once he showed him his pin of N'bane he was allowed into their great hall.  The tier'dal that guarded the fortress were a remnant of the warlike N'vinn, N'Tan and D'Ruggla families.  They'd guarded this spot of Rathe for centuries and rumor had it that that what they really protected was a secret tunnel that went all the way back to Neriak.  The outpost was mostly a training ground for the younger elves of the families with a few elders in the mix to keep things running smoothly.  The few elves out here from other clans  had been banished and were putting in time out here hoping to get reinstated with their clan lords.   The elder tier'dal offered him a place to sleep by the fire and a bowl of stew in exchange for any information about Neriak.  Some of them had been banished for centuries and any stories of tidbits he could tell them was welcome.  Vilamil wasn't much of a story teller but he updated them as best he could all the while seeding them with information about Kaldarye.
<br />

<br />
Most of them had not heard or had any encounters with the Nerksis but the Ka'Ducues clan was not unknown.  There was a mad necromancer, wandering the hills, they said that once bore the Ka'Duceus surname.  Vilamil filed that information away in his brain and wondered if the necromancer was any relation to Greon.   
<br />

<br />
Vilamil was not used to sleeping in crowded quarters, but he was glad he chose to spend the night inside. Though the outside of the keep was a sorry site, the inside was warm and comfortable.  The great hall was lined with long tables.   Torches were set into the wall and several pallets surrounded two huge fireplaces.  The smell of roasting meat filled the air and the wine was aplenty.  The younger Tier'dal hung on his every word while the older ones pressed him for information back in Neriak.  They had particular interest in which clans were in power as well as any new magic that was discovered.   Apparently news to this remote part of the world was very slow and they treated him like he was a returning war hero.    
<br />
 
<br />

<br />
Later as the sun rose behind the mountains and the stars glimmered in a fading night, Vilamil was given another hearty meal of duckling with wild onions as well as directions to the mountain pass to Lake Rathe  He left the fortress in good spirits confident he'd be back in Neriak in a few days.   On his way out of the mountains he  stopped by some gypsy tents to replenish his supplies. 
<br />

<br />
The gypsies eyed him distrustfully and pointed their combine weapons at him.  
<br />

<br />
“Go away dark elf your kind isn't wanted here.” a young man with curly black hair said.
<br />

<br />
Feigning surprise, Vilamil replied.  “But I'm not what you think.” 
<br />

<br />
“I know what you are.  Your a fiend of Innoruuk.” an older man spat.
<br />

<br />
“Nay your wrong.  I merely appear to be a minion of Innoruuk.  I'm really a half-elf.  My mother hailed from Qeynos.”
<br />

<br />
“Do you take me for a fool?” the old man spat on the ground.  “Next you'll tell me you worship Karana.”
<br />

<br />
Putting the most sublime smile on his face he could imagine Vilamil replied, “Actually, I do.”
<br />

<br />
“I know what I see and your skin is all inkie.” the old man replied.
<br />

<br />
“An unwanted gift from my father.” Vilamil frowned.  “It has caused a lot of problems in my lifetime.  When I was a child the priests in Qeynos once tried to bleach the dark elf from my skin.”  He shuddered as if remembering an unpleasant memory then continued,  “But the good people of Qeynos know what I really am.” he said reaching into his bag.  “See I have a badge of honor from that good city to prove it.” he said pulling out a small medallion.”
<br />

<br />
The older gypsie studied the token with surprise then lowered his weapon and said.  “Alright, half-elf.  What do you need.”
<br />

<br />
“Iron rations.” Vilamil replied.
<br />

<br />
They sold him a stack of rations for a prohibitive price.  Vilamil noticed them giving each other sly glances and smiles as he looked over their meager wares, but in keeping with his disguise he pretended he didn't notice.
<br />

<br />
After leaving the gypsies he headed towards the Lake Rathe pass out of the mountains.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br />Kaldarye Ka'Duceus
