				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Bridgid finally gave up following the strange Tier'dal looking half-breed when she realized he wanted nothing to do with her.  Going back to Devin she found him groaning on the path.  At least he was awake.  She knelt down on the ground and ran her fingers along his jaw.  Purple and black bruises mottled his skin.  He had a week's worth of beard and the skin surrounding his eyes was black.  When she got no response to her gentle stroking, she moved her hands to his chest and shook him.  Still getting no reaction she leaned over and smothered his bruised face in kisses.  
<br />

<br />
“Darling you have to get up, hurry we've just been rescued by another half-elf.” she said.
<br />

<br />
With a dazed expression, Devin opened his swollen eyes.  He looked at her and smiled, returned her embrace and said, “I've died and gone to Karana.”
<br />

<br />
“No.  You barely escaped death at the hands of the dark elves."  She pulled away from him.  "Come on get up, we've got to get out of here.”  Bridgid replied tugging on his sleeve and helping him off the ground.  
<br />

<br />
Devin unsteadily got to his feet and let her assist him onto the back of a pony.  He vaguely wondered what happened to his oxen and remembering the evil brigands, groaned at the memories.  "Where are we?" he said as he blinked in the early morning sun.  It was bright and there were scrubby mountains and corpses of Tier'dal elves littered around them.  “Whoa.. what happened.” he said as he took in the carnage.
<br />

<br />
“A long story.  I'll tell you later.  Come on we have to hurry.” she said as she led the pony down a steep path out of the mountains towards the fading light of Endah.   
<br />

<br />
“Wait.” Devin said drawing up the reins. “You should be riding, not me, you're the one with child.”  
<br />

<br />
“I'm fine.  The babe is still a few more days in coming.  Besides it's too dangerous for both of us to ride on this steep trail."
<br />

<br />
"But you're a pregnant woman.  It's not proper." he said as he started to dismount.  He was still in High Pass guard mode and him riding and her walking didn't seem right.
<br />

<br />
Bridgid walked to the side of the pony and put her hand on his leg.  "Stay up there Dev.  You don't look good.   You just woke up after being asleep for days."  
<br />

<br />
"What if someone sees us?"
<br />

<br />
She laughed.  "Who's gonna see us here."  Her voice echo'd off the reddish peaks.
<br />

<br />
She was right.  Devin felt sheepish.   
<br />

<br />
She turned and glanced up at him.  "When we get to flat land near the lake I'll climb up and ride with you.”   She gave him one of her heart stopping smiles before she turned back to the steep trail and continued leading the pony.
<br />

<br />
Devin stared at her backside marveling how she'd suddenly become self sufficient.  A few days ago when they'd left High Pass she was all but useless. Now having newly escaped the Tier'dal and standing amidst so many fresh corpses, she appeared ready to take on the world.  He wondered what had effected this change.   His memories of the past few days were vague and he blinked his eyes trying to fit together the pieces.  Thinking made his head hurt.  It was much easier to watch the grand swell of her belly and her gently swinging hips as she pulled the pony down the trail, then to figure out the details of the past several days.   Besides he felt dizzy and most of his joints ached.  
<br />

<br />
Bridgid led the pony to a lake and hesitated at the shores.  She started to lead the animal into the water but changed her mind.  She looked both ways along the shore before climbing up onto the pony's back.  Taking the reins from him she steered the beast towards a set of small footprints in the sand.
<br />

<br />
"Who's footprints are those?" Devin asked.
<br />

<br />
"Our benefactors."  She clicked the reins.  "And now it's our turn to return the favor."
<br />

<br />
Vilamil was cleaning his wounds in the shelter of a rock overhand when he saw the girl and the pony.  Only now she had her human with her.  He thought he could hide from them but realized too late they were following his trail in the sand.   They were nearly upon him before she stopped the beast. 
<br />

<br />
She looked at him with an expression pinched in concern.  “We can help you if you want. Two half-elves and a human shouldn't be alone in this wild land.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil's head snapped up.  Half-elf? This girl seriously thought he was a half elf?  Innoruuk's eye teeth what sort of trickery were the gods up to.   He would not have been more surprised had Bristlebane himself shown up to admonish him for worshipping Innoruuk.  He narrowed his eyes.  He was about to correct her unreal assumption but  a spiral of nausea rose in his gut.  As he swallowed the sickness back down he changed his mind about correcting her erronous assumption.   He studied the girl and her companion's beleaguered appearance.  If she thought he was a half elf then she saw him as an ally.   She had no way of knowing he was a tier'dal assassin.  His expression softened.  These two would be completely and unknowingly at his mercy.   If they thought he was a half breed why not continue the ruse.  He'd taken on other guises before.  Why not add this one to his repetoire.  These two might prove useful.  If not. He could always kill them later.
<br />

<br />
“I usually travel alone.” he answered guardedly.
<br />

<br />
“Well that's not very smart.  There's more safety in numbers.” she replied, “Besides you don't look as if you're going far for a while.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil was sorry he hadn't vomited all over her.  Who was this girl to tell him how to travel.  He'd taken care of himself longer than she'd been alive.  It took most of his patience to keep his expression semi-friendly.  “Can you heal?” he asked her.
<br />

<br />
“No.” she shook her head.  “But Devin might be able to make you a poultice.  He's a ranger.”
<br />

<br />
Devin coughed.  “Not a very good one.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil's eyes flashed.  Maybe gaining their trust was a mistake.  What in Innoruuk's name were they doing way out here in Rathe Mountains where the Nerksis had caught them.  He didn't feel comfortable traveling Rathe at his level of experience and he'd been to all of the outer planes.  These two had no business being anywhere outside of a well guarded city.
<br />

<br />
“Where are you from.” Vilamil asked them.
<br />

<br />
“High Keep.” Devin replied.
<br />

<br />
Vilamil frowned.  “Long way.”
<br />

<br />
“We were on our way to Surefall” Devin replied.
<br />

<br />
“Even further.”  Vilamil said.
<br />

<br />
“You know the way to Surefall?” Devin looked hopeful as if the rest of his life depended on Vilamil's answer.  
<br />

<br />
“North.  Then west, past Qeynos.   Then North again.”  Vilamil replied hoping his answer would get rid of them.  
<br />

<br />
To his disappointment the female dismounted.  She looked at the human she called Devin and said, “We need to find food.  Devin, darling, do you think you could forage some.”
<br />

<br />
“Aye,  if you'll stay here and get us a fire going.” the human called Devin answered.
<br />

<br />
“No fires.” Vilamil said.  At their questioning looks he added. “ We don't know what's out here.  We don't want to attract any unwanted attention, not while two of us aren't feeling well.”
<br />

<br />
Too his relief the two lighties agreed and while the female watched him  examine his wounds from the combat,  the male foraged.  A short time later the male came back with some roots and plants.  “This should hold us over the day,” he said as he offered the lions share of the vegetation to the pregnant female.    
<br />

<br />
Not commenting on the meager portion of foraged plants he'd been offered, Vilmil looked through his packs for something to neutralize the nauseating poison.  He only had a few pieces of ginger.  Turning to the "ranger" he tossed the ginger to him and said, “Find me that. It's a root called ginger.”  
<br />

<br />
“I don't know if I can.” Devin answered looking at the root uneasily. 
<br />

<br />
“Rangers forage.  Make yourself useful and Do it.” Vilamil snapped. 
<br />

<br />
Devin turned the root over in his hands.  “What is it?”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil gave him an annoyed glance.  “Ginger. It grows in the Karana's which are on the other side of this lake.  The ground here in Rathe should be close enough.  And while your out get yourself a bow and arrows off one of the dead dark elves.” he threw a dagger in between Devin's feet as extra incentive.
<br />

<br />
Devin looked at Bridgid and nodded as he stumbled out of the cave.   Bridgid watched him go and then she turned on Vilamil.  “You don't have to be so mean, just because you are handy with a dagger.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil glared at her.  His patience and his benevolence were running thin.  He retrieved his dagger out of the sand.  “I told you to leave and you followed me here.  Now deal with my temper or get out.”
<br />

<br />
She glared at him.  “I'd leave if we could but we can't.”
<br />

<br />
When he didn't respond she continued.  “We don't know the way back.”
<br />

<br />
She glanced at him to see if he was listening but he'd gone back to tending his wounds.  “And we can't defend ourselves.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil heard her last remark as he pressed a dagger into his skin and used it to dig out the shaft of the arrow.  He winced as the barbed tip was pulled free and then poured a liberal amount of water over the wound to clean it.   Once the stinging subsided he bound the wound with a bandage.
<br />

<br />
“How can you do that to yourself.  Doesn't it hurt?” Bridgid asked concerned.
<br />

<br />
Vilamil glanced at her as he applied a bandage to stop the bleeding. “Necessary.”
<br />

<br />
She watched fascinated as he did the same with another arrow wound, noticing his skin had no trace of mottling that hers did.  His skin was flawlessly black and smooth.  She wondered if he knew how lucky he was to have inherited most of his Tier'dal ancestry.   She wished she had been born full tier'dal.  The dark elves led a life that was mysterious and exciting.  And she wondered how much on the edge of darkness he was able to live.
<br />

<br />
When he finished removing the two arrows he cleaned the smaller nicks and cuts that he'd sustained in combat.   He seemed to know what he was doing, and Bridgid's ideas of cleaning his wounds, taking care of him and making him grateful for their company evaporated as the sensation that they needed him a lot more than he needed them sank in.  Hopefully there was something she could do make herself useful.  But all her talents were in song and dancing and she had a feeling that this dark one wouldn't be interested in her dramatics. 
<br />

<br />
Devin returned a short while later without any success in locating ginger.  The dark one took the news without any expression and instead asked Devin if he had any knowledge of fishing.
<br />

<br />
“A little.” Devin admitted.  “We'd fish off the shore of the lake in the glade when we were younger.”  
<br />

<br />
“Find me a staff about as tall as yourself and bring it to me.” he ordered.
<br />

<br />
Even though Devin felt terrible, he did as he was told.  He recognized the voice of authority in the Dark One and instinctively knew that one person able to slay so many tier'dal back on the mountain trail was not someone they wanted as an enemy.   When he returned with a staff,  the Dark One pulled out a ball of twine from one of his many pockets and made a crude fishing pole that he handed back to  Devin.   He pointed towards he lake and then backed deep into the overhang.   
<br />

<br />
Bridgid turned away from the Dark one assuming he was going to sleep.  She was tired too.  Perhaps a little nap wouldn't hurt.  
<br />

<br />
When she awoke the sun was sinking and Devin was cleaning the single fish he'd spent the day catching.   The dark one was missing from the overhang.  Alarmed, Bridgid looked at Devin and said, “where is he?”
<br />

<br />
“He went fishing.  That was after saying a bunch of things to me in some other language.  I'm not sure but I think he was swearing.”  
<br />

<br />
“He probably was. He's not a very nice man.”
<br />

<br />
“Man? Hah.  He's an elf.”
<br />

<br />
“Well a half elf.” Bridgid corrected.
<br />

<br />
Devin shook his head.  “There's no way that's a halfie.  Look at him. If he's half-tier dal he's all elf.  One of his parents was probably a woody.”
<br />

<br />
“A woody eh?” She stared back at him and smiled looking suddenly all innocent and seductive.  
<br />

<br />
Devin sidled closer and ran his fingers through her yellowish hair, noticing that her white roots were showing. He leaned close to her ear and whispered, “I've wanted to hold you and touch you since the moment I woke up.”  He nibbled her pointy ear as he massaged her scalp in slow lazy circles.  “You're the reason I woke up.  Do you know that?”
<br />

<br />
“No.” she giggled, a silly laugh but to him it sounded like raw passion.
<br />

<br />
“Every time I regained consciousness there you were, feeding me, talking to me, smiling at me.” Devin continued as he ran his tongue down her neck.
<br />

<br />
“You were so bruised.” she kissed his face lingering on one of the old bruises beneath his eye.
<br />

<br />
Beneath her his muscles flexed.
<br />

<br />
“Does that hurt?” she asked as she felt him tense.
<br />

<br />
He pulled her close intending to show her exactly what hurt and how she could make it better when a bundle of dead eyed fish dropped at his feet.
<br />

<br />
“Cook those.” the Dark One said as he moved back into the cavern.  
<br />

<br />
Devin glared at the elf.  He couldn't believe the rotten timing.  And how had this elf caught so many fish so quickly.  Karana must be frowning.  “How?  You told us not to build a fire.”
<br />

<br />
“You can build one here.” he gestured a spot under a small outcropping in the roof.  “There's a natural flue in the cavern you can used to dissipate the smoke.”
<br />

<br />
“Why didn't you tell us this earlier?” Devin asked unable to hide his frustration.
<br />

<br />
A silence fell between him and the elf.   Devin could see barely controlled fury skating across the Tier'dal features as the dark one replied, “Most rangers know how to build a smokeless fire and they'd notice the natural flu in the rock long before I would.”
<br />

<br />
“I told you, I'm not a very good ranger.”  Devin replied. 
<br />

<br />
Again the dark one did not reply. He gave Devin an angry glare with his bright gray eyes then nodded towards the stone flue before retreating back into the cave.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br />Kaldarye Ka'Duceus
<br />
