				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Over the next several days they circumvented Lake Rathe, traveling along it's outer shores back towards North Karana.   Devin gradually got better at fishing after several trial and error attempts.  At first he was better at catching old shoes than fish.  Bridgid thought it was funny, especially when he produced her a soggy matched set of silk sandals; the Dark One said nothing.  
<br />

<br />
When the reached the valley to the Karana, Bridgid thought for sure the Dark One would abandon them but he crossed through the narrow passage and led them out onto the  Southern Plains.  His wounds looked nearly healed but he was still forcing Devin to forage ginger, which told her he wasn't as well as he was leading them to believe.  She kept her thoughts to herself, as she tried to keep up with him.  Even though he was on foot and they were on horseback he ran at a constant pace.  She wished he'd slow down.  The constant trotting was upsetting to her baby.  
<br />

<br />
Like they had in Lake Rathe, they followed the wall of the plain's edge not crossing directly through it.  They traveled with Endah on their  left into the even vaster plains of Western Karana, where again she thought he would leave them, but once again he stayed.  It was a good thing too, for they ran into more bandits but the Dark One easily repelled them.
<br />

<br />
After the attack while he was checking over their corpses, Bridgid glanced at him and said, “For as mean as you are, you're a good ranger.”
<br />

<br />
“Ranger?” the Dark One said in disbelief.  He laughed. Bridgid still had no idea what his name was and he wasn't volunteering it, but this was first time she'd ever seen him show any emotion other than scowling.
<br />

<br />
Devin gave her a warning glance but she unabashedly continued.  “Yes.  You have to be a ranger, whether you admit it or not.  I've seen the way you fight and fish and build fires.  You found the way all the way here to Western Karana without a map.”
<br />

<br />
“I doubt the people of your  glade will be waiting to welcome me as a ranger.” he replied as he pried a sword out of the grasp of one of the dead bandits.  He tested the weapon in the air as if judging it's weight and balance then he unstrapped the bandit's belt and handed both sword and scabbard to Devin.
<br />

<br />
“Well no. but that's because of the way you appear.  Face it.  You look like an evil dark elf.”
<br />

<br />
“I do.” he replied.
<br />

<br />
“And your attitude isn't the best.” she added.  "If you would only smile more, people would think better of you."
<br />

<br />
He gave her a scathing glance.  "Yet you would call me a ranger.”  he said as he gestured for Devin to equip the sword.
<br />

<br />
“Well I know that you're not evil.” She smiled encouragingly.   “Once we introduce you to Devin's people they will like you too, won't they Dev.”
<br />

<br />
Devin just grunted.  He was feeling a little jealous.  Bridgid's attitude towards this Dark One was becoming a little too interested.  The way she watched him and tilted her head as if she were speculating how he'd be as a lover.  The sooner they got away from this odd dark skinned elf the better.  There was something about him that made Devin uneasy.  Something about the way he cleanly killed the bandits without any feeling or remorse.   
<br />

<br />
Vilamil looked at her amused but inside he wondered if he was going mad.  Had he really played his roll as a half-breed that well?  He should have killed them both back on the shores of Lake Rathe.   He should have known better than to pretend to be friends with the lighties.  Now what was he going to do with them once he got them into the valley of Qeynos.  Release them, let them continue on their way to Surefall content they'd been rescued by a friendly half-elven black-skinned ranger.   He mulled it over in his head.  The idea was entertaining, and he didn't see what harm it would bring.  
<br />

<br />
Glancing at Bridgid, he wondered how soon her child would come.  To him she looked gigantic, even bigger than Kaldarye had been when she birthed Keeah, and definitely less maintenance.    Kaldarye had been a typical wicked, foul-mooded tier'dal female which only softened a little when Keeah had arrived.  In truth he'd expected her to kill Keeah as soon as she was born but she'd surprised him by taking the babe to her breast.   He had no doubt that Bridgid would do the same.  Bridgid would probably make an excellent mother to a lightie.  His eyes filled with melancholy as suddenly he was flooded with bittersweet memories of his other life on Legends.   He missed Kaldarye, Keeah and his own kind.  There had been something idyllic and worry-free about living in that other realm.  His only concern on Legends had been trying to get Kaldarye's face repaired.  And even though he'd been mostly unsuccessful in getting her healed, life there had been sublime compared to the chaotic Rallos.
<br />

<br />
Bridgid noticed the change in his expression and she said, “What's wrong?”
<br />

<br />
“Old memories.” He answered.
<br />

<br />
“How old are you?”
<br />

<br />
“Older than your grandparents.” he answered truthfully.
<br />

<br />
“Oh I don't know about that.  My father was a dark elf.”
<br />

<br />
“So was mine.” he answered.
<br />

<br />
Then we have something in common.  We're kindred spirits traveling the night.”
<br />

<br />
He gave her a disbelieving glance.  Was she out of her mind?  No matter what he said or what happened to them she somehow managed to find some sort of connection.  He was beginning to think, despite her obvious Tier'dal appearance, that her sire couldn't possibly be one of his people.   If Kaldarye were here, Bridgid would be on a makeshift alter of Innoruuk so fast there wouldn't be time to cry for help.   
<br />
 
<br />
His fingers caressed his dagger.  He missed tier'dal cruelty and the way of life he knew and understood.  These two had gotten too close.  He didn't like the idea that they knew something about him even if their thoughts were in error.   Compassion was weakness.   Hadn't he always been taught that?   
<br />

<br />
Nausea bubbled in his throat again and he removed his fingers from his dagger to reach for ginger.  At least the ranger's forage skills had been somewhat useful.  It was annoying not having any malady healed by a cleric.  He was glad this journey to the portal book of Qeynos was almost over.   But whatever god was protecting these two from his natural tier'dal instincts had a final surprise.  Suddenly Bridgid doubled over with her hand on her belly and cried out in pain.
<br />

<br />
“What's wrong?” Devin said as he supported her with one hand under her elbow.  She screamed and cried out again.  
<br />

<br />
“The baby. I think it's coming.”
<br />

<br />
They moved quickly through the rest of Western Karana and crossed over into the farmhills of Qeynos just before dawn.  The Dark One found a  skeleton of a barn on an abandoned field and led her inside.  Bridgid was soaking wet, from the water of her baby.   
<br />

<br />
“What do we do.” Devin cried as he helped her off the pony and laid her against a bale of moldy straw.
<br />

<br />
“I do nothing.  You ride to the nearest farm and get help.” The Dark One said as he handed the reins over to Devin.
<br />

<br />
“What, I can't leave my wife.  You take the horse and get help.”
<br />

<br />
The Dark One grabbed him and shook him.  “Look in my eyes, at my face.  Do you think the people here would trust?  Now ride and make haste.”
<br />

<br />
Nodding Devin took the reins and galloped off.  Vilamil watched him go and then looked down at Bridgid.  He wondered if Devin would return in time to witness the birth of his child.  Gathering his things. He started to leave.  But the damnable female grabbed his leg.  
<br />

<br />
“Wait don't leave me.  Where are you going?”
<br />

<br />
“Home.” he replied as he shook his leg free. 
<br />

<br />
“Home?  You can't leave me.”
<br />

<br />
“You'll be fine.  You're strong.”
<br />

<br />
“Wait.   Where is your home?  When will we see you again.” she cried between hard contractions.
<br />

<br />
“You won't.” he replied as he took one last look at her and slipped out into the night.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br />Kaldarye Ka'Duceus
<br />
