				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody"><span style="font-size: 18px; line-height: normal"><span style="font-weight: bold">Neriak</span></span>
<br />

<br />

<br />
House N'bane was in the midst of a celebration.   The manor's entrance  was swathed in dried grapevine and golden glow stones, and every window was lit with a small candle.  Two lions roasted over a spit near the outer gate, their drippings making popping and hissing sounds as their juices fell to the coals.  A crowd of young Tier'dal watched in wide eyed awe as two trolls turned the feline meat skewers in slow circles.  The meat smelled greasy but Vilamil knew it would be soaked in spices and cherry brine and decorated with a variety of roots and vegetables before being brought to the table.  
<br />

<br />
Slipping past the crowd he entered the house.   The walls of the great hall were hung with torquoise silk worked intricately with black and gold with pthallic patterns.   The torquoise, gold and black on the walls  was reflected in the striped chairs, the tablecloth, and platform erected near the front of the room. Along the perimeter servants  were setting up platters of cockatrice, olives, garlic, apples, eggplant, drachnid legs and  boiled drake eggs in artful towers on several long tables.   The air smelled of rich red wine, boiling meats and expensive leather.  A lesser house with a green crest that Vilamil didn't recognize was circling the banquet and mingling with the nobles of  clan N'bane.  Vilamil circled around the edge of the room and listened to their conversation.    It didn't take long to determine the celebration was a wedding ceremony,  for one of the house  females.  About two years ago he had once over heard D'JAren tell his brother Baellor that a strong house always had a good supply of lesser noble females to marry off and make alliances.   
<br />

<br />
Not seeing his clanlord at the celebration, Vilamil headed towards D'Jaren's private quarters.  On his way he passed the room of the bride and learned that she was not so willing.  He peered in on her to see who it was and grinned when he saw it was Valatha, a cruel female who was one of Baellor's daughters.  Vilamil had never liked Valatha.  When he was much younger an inexperienced, she'd once sent him on a fruitless search to najena for dry bone powder; it nearly got him killed - a deed he had never repaid her for.   He hoped the match to the clan in green was far beneath her.    
<br />

<br />
“I won't do it.”
<br />

<br />
“You will or you'll be handed to the necros for target practice.” her mother replied as she pulled tight the strings on the blue wedding kirtle.
<br />

<br />
“You're mad if you think I'm going to marry so far beneath my station.” Valatha countered.
<br />

<br />
“Then I'm mad.” her mother replied.
<br />

<br />
“House V'Karissia isn't even noble.” the girl grunted and the dress was tightened to the point she could barely breathe.    
<br />

<br />
“It doesn't matter.”
<br />

<br />
She dropped her hands to her waist and attempted to loosen the strings. “Pr'Manthis is six-hundred years my senior.” 
<br />

<br />
“Age matters not.  You'll marry him because your clan lord has ordered it.” 
<br />

<br />
“He won't be my clan lord after today.” she replied with a defiant glare and a toss of her braided hair.
<br />

<br />
Her mother slapped her.  “Don't speak such foolishness.  D'Jaren will always be your clanlord.  Unless you marry the king.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha recoiled from the slap and rubbed her cheek where the imprint of her mother's palm lingered. “I'm only speaking truth.”
<br />

<br />
“Forget the truth.”
<br />

<br />
“Truth is all I have.” Valatha insisted.
<br />

<br />
“We are Children of Innoruuk.  Truth matters not.”
<br />

<br />
“No mother it does.  The truth is I don't want to marry down and I'm being forced too by an elflord who thinks he can rule my life.”
<br />

<br />
“He does rule your life and you'll do what he asks.  Whatever he asks and when he asks.  Your life is his.  Don't forget it.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha tugged at the strings on her waist.  “It's too tight.  Loosen it up some.”
<br />

<br />
“You can have it loosened by the V'Karissia slaves once your mistress of your own household.”
<br />

<br />
“I don't want to be mistress of the V'Karissia household.  Where's the benefit to me marrying into the merchant class?”
<br />

<br />
“Plenty when you consider how shrewd in business they are.  You could have done much worse marrying a poor noble with a shack for a home.”
<br />

<br />
“At least marrying an impoverished noble would still make me a lady.”
<br />

<br />
“Aye, but a poor one.”
<br />

<br />
“Poor would still grant me access to the better parties.”
<br />

<br />
“You'd have no access anywhere wearing rags.”
<br />

<br />
“Pishhh.  I have plenty of money of my own.”
<br />

<br />
“Only because your clanlord is generous.”  Her mother grabbed her chin and forced her to look into her eyes.  “You are nothing without this family.  You'd do well to remember that.”
<br />

<br />
“Who I am related to will matter naught once I'm forced to enter a lesser clan.”
<br />

<br />
“The V'Karissia clan is wealthy.  Your situation could be worse.”
<br />

<br />
“Worse?  How could this be worse, mother?”
<br />

<br />
Her mother released her chin.  “Look at the clan lords own daughter.  How would you like to be shackled to an elf like that.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha's eyes glared angrily like churning amethyst pools.  “At least Kaldarye chose who she married.”
<br />

<br />
“Against D'Jaren's will.  She will pay for it.  No one angers your uncle and gets away with it.” her mother said as she straightened out the folds on the dress.   
<br />

<br />
Valatha scowled.  “Kaldarye has already gotten away with it.  She's his own flesh.  He won't harm a hair on her head.”
<br />

<br />
“Her flesh won't make her immune.  D'jaren is very patient.  He never forgets.”
<br />

<br />
“I still think she's luckier.  I would gladly become Greon's second wife if it would get me out of marrying Pr'Manthis.” 
<br />

<br />
Her mother slapped her again.  “You will go to the wedding and do what D'jaren bids.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha glared at her but held her tongue as her mother continued, “You will marry this merchant elf.  Then you will fade from society and  stay out of D'Jaren's notice. Make yourself useful while your mistress of his estate.  Learn what you can about your husband's business.   Bide your time and wait.”
<br />

<br />
“Wait?  For what?”
<br />

<br />
Her mother's eyes glinted with cunning.  “For Kaldarye's demise.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha tossed her head back and laughed.  Her creamy braids bounced on her shoulders.  “Who's stupid enough to kill her?  No one will dare stand up to N'bane.”
<br />

<br />
Her mother's face twisted with a wry smile.  “You need to be more observant.  Trust me, when the hour comes that Kaldarye is no longer useful,  you will see her tumble.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha folded her arms over her chest.  “She's his child.  Unless he has another, I can't see how her situation would change for the worse.  Even if Kaldarye runs off and marries a tavern worker at the Blind Fish, she's still D'jaren's offspring.”
<br />

<br />
“Kaldarye's usefulness ends when she produces an acceptable heir to our clan.” her mother said as she directed a slave to repair a small rent near the hem of the dress.
<br />

<br />
“Acceptable.” Valatha frowned.  “You're the fool if you think D'jaren will accept  a child born of Ka'Duceus?”
<br />

<br />
“He will if he has no choice.”
<br />

<br />
“And what then?  Even if Kaldarye somehow dies,  we'll have another D'Jaren seedling to deal with when it grows up.”
<br />

<br />
“Not if it never grows up.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha rolled her eyes.  “Mother you know as well as I that any future heir to this house will be raised under an army of nannies and guards.”
<br />

<br />
“There are ways to cut a life short. And if D'Jaren's heir doesn't live, the mantle of the clan falls next on your father.”
<br />

<br />
“That won't help me.  I'll still be married to no name V'Karissia.”  
<br />

<br />
“Your alliance can be dissolved once your father is lord.”
<br />

<br />
“Why would father do that?  He doesn't even like me.  He thinks I'm vain and petty.  He told me as much yesterday when it was announced I was getting married.”
<br />

<br />
Her mother's eyes looked cunning. “You're his eldest.”
<br />

<br />
“A lot of good that's done me.”
<br />

<br />
Her mother grabbed her hands and squeezed them til it hurt.  “Think, Valatha.  For once think about something other than yourself.  You're the eldest offspring.   That makes you his heir.” 
<br />

<br />
Valatha's lips puckered with scorn.  “Mother you are mad.  You're placating speech won't work.  I know better than to believe such nonsense.  There isn't any reason why a child of Kaldarye wouldn't live to adulthood.”
<br />

<br />
“It won't live when it fails the test of purity.”
<br />

<br />
“I thought uncle Mittan tried that with Kaldarye.  He tried to make her fail the blood temple's test and she passed.  Innoruuk himself saved her.”
<br />

<br />
“And look what she's made of herself?  She's become an outcast to our clan.  And we are the largest clan in Neriak.  We, the children of Innoruuk are being made to look like fools.  Innoruuk won't make the same mistake twice.”   
<br />

<br />
“You are assuming Innoruuk made a mistake.  What if Innoruuk has plans you haven't yet fathomed.”
<br />

<br />
“Your father is a priest.  He would know if that were the case.” Her mother said as she put a spiked mail skirt around Valatha's waist. The chain mail was made of delicate silver filigree with the spikes facing inwards digging into the dress.  Each spike had been tipped with poison so that any casual brushing against her skin caused intense pain.  “Now come we must hurry we are running late.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha refused to budge and her mother added, “Make even the slightest protest at the wedding and I'll beat these spikes into your skin till the pain makes you writhe and wish for death.”
<br />

<br />
Valatha's response was to curl her inky hands into fists.  “You cant force me to marry him.”
<br />

<br />
“Oh yes I can.” her mother said pushing the barbed skirt into Valatha's flesh causing her to scream.
<br />

<br />
Having heard enough, Vilamil continued towards D'Jaren's quarters.  Valatha's cries sounded in the hallway behind him.  It reminded him of a slow agonizing death of gnomes getting steamed alive in a troll's kitchen, each minute more horrible then the next until death finally claimed their souls.    He turned a corner, entered another hallway and the cries were muffled.  He was glad he wasn't important enough in his clan that D'Jaren had ever considered ordering him into an unwanted marriage.   But depending on how he played out this game with Kaldarye his importance in the clan could change.   He'd have to be careful when dealing with D'Jaren.  The last thing he wanted was for the clan liege to get the idea to try and take control of Vilamil's personal destiny.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br />Kaldarye Ka'Duceus
<br />
Ugly Lady of the Hovel
<br />
Dark Mistress of Innoruuk
<br />
