	<tr>
		<td width="150" align="left" valign="top" class="row1"><span class="name"><a name="23641"></a><b>Kaldarye</b></span><br /><span class="postdetails">Officers<br /><img src="images/avatars/gallery/Avatar_pack_1/568.gif" alt="" border="0" /><br /><br />Joined: 24 Apr 2004<br />Posts: 493<br /></span><br /></td>
		<td class="row1" width="100%" height="28" valign="top"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
			<tr>
				<td width="100%"><a href="viewtopic.php?p=23641&amp;sid=9dcf8711772a8a09b388f56c951f63dd#23641"><img src="templates/Classic/images/icon_minipost.gif" width="12" height="9" alt="Post" title="Post" border="0" /></a><span class="postdetails">Posted: Sat Dec 25, 2004 2:40 pm<span class="gen">&nbsp;</span>&nbsp; &nbsp;Post subject: </span></td>
				<td valign="top" align="right" nowrap="nowrap"><a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=23641&amp;sid=9dcf8711772a8a09b388f56c951f63dd"><img src="templates/Classic/images/lang_english/icon_quote.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>   </td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">A few minutes after D'jaren and Baellor had finished their conversation and left the blue  room, Vilamil slipped out of his hiding place and headed to the back of the keep to a little used hallway  leading to a hidden door.   The narrow hall wound around to the back of the manor to a steep flight of stairs that ended in a window leading out onto a ledge.   Once outside the house he stood on the ledge in the shadows of the manor and thought over what he'd heard.   
<br />

<br />
He now  knew it was getting dangerous to continue to keep Kaldarye at Greon's house.  It wouldn't be long before D'jaren went sniffing back there, either to send Greon a warning or to have the place thoroughly searched for his missing child.   And if the place was searched, Nilla, the il'haut tier'dal would be found which would inevitably lead to the knowledge that Vilamil had been to Legends.   Reflexively he curled and uncurled his fist  in frustration.  Perhaps taking the il'haut tier'dal instead of Keaah away from Malsqueak had been the wrong choice.   If D'jaren learned Kaldarye had been under his care on legends there'd be hell to pay and Vilamil wasn't sure his skills as a rogue could protect him from an elf with an arm as long as D'Jaren N'bane.  His eyes narrowed.  Of course if D'jaren didn't learn about Legends, there'd be a high reward to collect due to the pressure from the king.  
<br />

<br />
It was an interesting situation.   One with limitless intrigue.   Calmly Vilamil weighed the pros and cons of continuing to shelter Kaldarye  versus handing her off to D'jaren.   Keeping her depended heavily on Greon, but giving her to D'jaren would end his hand in the game.   Vilamil enjoyed being the silent hand in the thick of things.   He felt like a spider in the center of a web and it was not a position he wanted to relinquish.
<br />

<br />
The sound of voices coming from the front of the manor caught his attention and staying on the ledge he circled the walls to investigate, stopping only when he found a vantage point where he could see both the main gate and the guarded courtyard of Talon.   In the arched doorway of the manor, Valatha appeared in a stunning blue wedding kirtle followed by her new husband Pra'Manthis V'Karrissia.    They were followed by a small entourage of house V'Karrisia family members dressed in resplendant green and gold finery.
<br />

<br />
Vilamil watched the departure of the wedding goers without expression.  So the forced wedding D'jaren had arranged to displease the king was over.  His eyes roved over the pinched face of Valatha and her beaming common husband.   Valatha, the court favorite,  was no longer noble.  Vilamil figured the clanlord had bought himself a few weeks of scorn by Thex.  Which was time enough to consider his next move.  
<br />

<br />
He crouched down and watched the V'Karrissia procession leave the Talon main gate.   At this point he wasn't ready to turn Kaldarye in.  Not  yet.  Not with the temptation of earning a greater prize if he held out a little longer.   He was determined to throw his clanlord off her trail one more time in hopes that the pressure from Thex drove the reward to heavenly levels.   
<br />

<br />
Climbing down from his perch Vilamil returned to his spartan private quarters in Talon Village where he sent a message up to Talon letting D'jaren know he'd return from looking for Kaldarye.  It didn't take long to get a summons and by mid afternoon he was changed into a cobalt undertunic,  chainmail and a black tabbard.    The tabbard was made of soft boiled leather and studded with tiny silver talons.  He put on dark gray breeches and black boots, and slid well concealed daggers into hidden compartments. 
<br />

<br />
Two hours later he walked back into the blue room of  Talon, stopped in the center of the room, made a small cursory bow and looked at D'jaren.   The clanlord's icy eyes had speared him when he'd entered and were watching him still.   Vilamil kept his face utterly calm.  
<br />

<br />
“You've been gone a long time.”  D'jaren said after a few minutes.  The displeasure was clearing marked in his frown.
<br />

<br />
“I ran into trouble.”  Vilamil replied, purposefully trying to throw D'jaren off.  
<br />

<br />
And when he didn't elaborate D'jaren's sharp eyes narrowed and his voice carried a wealth of intimidation.  “Trouble?”
<br />

<br />
“With the Nerksis in Lake Rathe.”  Vilamil said thinking back briefly to his adventures with the two lighties from Surefall glade. 
<br />

<br />
D'jaren's scowl blackened.   “What about them?”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil purposefully hesitated to give D'jaren the sense there was something he'd rather not tell him.  “You will not be pleased.”
<br />

<br />
D'jaren's light blue eyes pierced into him. “What happened.” 
<br />

<br />
“The Nerksis attacked me.”
<br />

<br />
“Why do you involve yourself with Neriak politics when you're on a mission for me?” D'jaren said with a dangerous glint.
<br />

<br />
Keeping his gave on D'jaren's face, Vilamil continued, “It was not my choice, m'lord.  The Nerksis decided a  Tier'dal traveling alone in Lake Rathe had to be a  skanking half breed.”  
<br />

<br />
“You were accused of being half bred?”  D'jaren's shock was genuine.
<br />

<br />
Vilamil nodded. 
<br />

<br />
“Hates Touch.” D'jaren swore.  “Are they blind?”
<br />

<br />
“Young and greedy.”  Vilamil replied.  “And not very knowledgeable of our clan.  They scoffed at our House symbol.”
<br />

<br />
“I assume you killed them.”  D'jaren said as the scorn left his eyes, replaced by a predatory trace.
<br />

<br />
“Of course.  But I've made no friends with this house and their kind.” Vilamil replied.
<br />

<br />
“The Nerksis are unimportant.”
<br />

<br />
“They are everywhere and may come sniffing Talon when they hit their bind.”
<br />

<br />
“It doesn't matter.  I pay the temples well when they try to start trouble here.”  D'jaren replied as if it were a minor inconvenience.  “What about Kaldarye.  Any news of her?”
<br />

<br />
“Mayhap.”  Vilamil replied intentionally vague.
<br />

<br />
D'jaren leaned forward and Vilamil could see he had his full attention.
<br />

<br />
“I met a newcomer to our lands.” Vilamil continued as he started inventing a tale.  “He originally came from the Legendary outer plane of existence.   He suggested that Kaldarye could be escaping your detection by  living in another realm, and that her named sounded like something he'd heard in the lands of legends.”
<br />

<br />
D'Jaren's icy gaze flickered.  Then his eyes rounded and his features came together as if everything finally made sense.   He snapped his fingers. “Hate's Minions.   That hadn't occurred to me.  She must have left our realm.  That must be why I can't find her with magic.”
<br />

<br />
“Then m'lord all you have to do is send someone to legends to find her.”
<br />

<br />
D'jaren kept his gaze on Vilamil.  “You will go.   I would pay you very well, 10,000 pp for the trip.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil's heart sped up at the thought of more money.  “And if I find your daughter and return her?, My Lord.”
<br />

<br />
“I'll transfer 500,000 pp to your bank account.” his clanlord replied.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br />Kaldarye Ka'Duceus
<br />
Ugly Lady of the Hovel
<br />
Dark Mistress of Innoruuk
